KERNEL DTRACE COVERAGE: ELEVENTH EXPANSION
========================================

33 new contracts in six kernel components, extending two existing providers
(eventfd and timerfd) and introducing posixsem, giftunnel, gretunnel and
vxlanctl. Six diagnostic profiles are selected for installation in base.
This is one kernel-focused pass through all nine requested categories, not
a claim that every subsystem or every path has all nine kinds of probes.

Category mapping
----------------
lifecycle: posixsem allocate/release; giftunnel/gretunnel/vxlanctl control
latency: paired control, wait, sleep, copy and fd allocation
errors: paired results, semaphore post/limit, timer cancellation
resources: posixsem allocate/release/limit; timerfd fd allocation
io: eventfd/timerfd read copyout phases
network: GIF/GRE/VXLAN administrative controls
synchronization: eventfd read/write sleeps; timerfd read sleep; POSIX semaphore waits
access-control: semaphore DAC/privilege override result; tunnel privilege checks
counters-state: semaphore old/new values; eventfd read counter consumption; timerfd read reset

Detailed scope and argument semantics
-------------------------------------
POSIX semaphore allocation emits allocate(object, initial_value) only after
the object and MAC label have been initialized, before publication. release
emits the opaque identity before freeing the last-reference object. limit
emits the current global object count and teardown flag while the count lock
is held: it can mean the configured count limit or teardown, not malloc
failure. No instrumentation is inside the allocator or module initialization.

access(object, error) reports the final DAC/privilege-override decision in
ksem_access. It is not a complete MAC audit: MAC may reject before this helper.
wait-start(object, tryflag, timed) runs after successful descriptor lookup and
mutex acquisition and before the MAC check. wait-done(object, error) covers
MAC denial, try-wait EAGAIN, timeout, signal interruption and successful
consumption. It excludes failed descriptor lookup and initial lock wait.
The sleep pair surrounds each actual cv wait, including mutex reacquisition.
A timed sleep's EWOULDBLOCK may lead to another wait or final ETIMEDOUT; a
sleep completion is not proof that a semaphore token was acquired.
post(object, current_value, error) covers MAC denial, EOVERFLOW and successful
post after descriptor lookup. value(object, old, new, operation) records
successful post (operation 1) and consumption (0), under sem_lock. No names,
credentials, paths, labels or user payloads are exposed.

Eventfd read-wait and write-wait pairs cover each mtx_sleep and its actual
return value, including mutex reacquisition, outside any interrupt context.
read-state(object, old_count, new_count) records consumption under efd_lock.
The preexisting write probe already reports increments and is not duplicated.
read-copy pairs measure the uiomove phase after the counter has been consumed.
A copyout EFAULT does not undo counter consumption; these two events let a
trace distinguish consumption from successful delivery. Early invalid sizes,
nonblocking EAGAIN and copyin failures do not get these new pairs.

Timerfd read-wait/read-copy have the corresponding semantics. fd-alloc pairs
report flags and falloc error, including descriptor exhaustion, in the create
syscall path after flag/clock validation; no uninitialized fd is exposed.
read-reset(object, prior_jump_state, discarded_count, error) fires under the
object lock before a read clears a pending clock-jump state and count. The
error may be zero or ECANCELED. This adds no site to the expiration callout,
clock-adjustment path or early clock setup.

GIF/GRE control pairs cover the complex ioctl branch from immediately before
acquiring the ioctl sx lock through the final result, before releasing it.
They include lock wait, copyin/out, configuration and errors (including a
missing softc). Args are interface identity, ioctl command, and final errno.
Early MTU/address/multicast/unsupported-command returns remain outside this
pair. Netlink configuration paths are not claimed. access emits the result
of each explicit GIF/GRE privilege check for FIB/options/key/port changes.
Neither keys nor addresses are exposed.

VXLAN control pairs surround only a validated driver-specific callback.
They exclude preceding validation/copyin and following copyout. access emits
the explicit privilege decision before callback dispatch. Generic VXLAN
ioctls and packet encapsulation/decapsulation are not newly instrumented.
All tunnel identity arguments are opaque: profiles never dereference them.

